<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Bánh> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "In [Vietnamese](:Vietnamese_language), the term ***bánh*** (Template:IPA-vi or Template:IPA-vi, [Chữ Nôm](:Ch%E1%BB%AF_N%C3%B4m): 餅) translates loosely as \"cake\" or \"bread\", but refers to a wide variety of prepared foods that can easily be eaten by hands or [chopsticks](:Chopsticks). With the addition of qualifying adjectives, *bánh* refers to a wide variety of sweet or savory, distinct cakes, buns, pastries, sandwiches, and other food items, which may be cooked by steaming, baking, frying, deep-frying, or boiling. Foods made from [wheat flour](:Wheat_flour) or [rice flour](:Rice_flour) are generally called *bánh*, but the term may also refer to certain varieties of noodle and fish cake dishes, such as *[bánh canh](:B%C3%A1nh_canh)* and *[bánh hỏi](:B%C3%A1nh_h%E1%BB%8Fi)*.Each variety of *bánh* is designated by a descriptive word or phrase that follows the word *bánh*, such as *[bánh bò](:B%C3%A1nh_b%C3%B2)* (lit. 'cow cake') or *[bánh chuối](:B%C3%A1nh_chu%E1%BB%91i)* (lit. 'banana cake'). *Bánh* that are wrapped in leaves before steaming are called *[bánh lá](:B%C3%A1nh_l%C3%A1)* (lit. 'leaf cakes').In Vietnamese, the term **bánh** is not limited to [Vietnamese cuisine](:Vietnamese_cuisine): it applies equally to items as varied as [fortune cookies](:Fortune_cookie) (**bánh may mắn**), [pudding](:Pudding) (**bánh pudding*, *bánh pútđinh**), [caramel custard](:Cr%C3%A8me_caramel) (**bánh caramen*, *bánh flan**), [sacramental bread](:Sacramental_bread) (**Bánh Thánh**), [Hamburger](:Hamburger) (**bánh Hamburger*, *bánh Hămbơgơ**), etc.In some cases, the word can also refer to inedibles that have a cake-like shape, such as car tires (**bánh xe**), bath soaps, and compressed tobacco wheels."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Beef_Wellington> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Beef Wellington is a steak dish of English origin, made out of fillet steak coated with pâté (often pâté de foie gras) and duxelles, wrapped in puff pastry, then baked. Some recipes include wrapping the coated meat in a crêpe or parma ham to retain the moisture and prevent it from making the pastry soggy. A whole tenderloin may be wrapped and baked, and then sliced for serving, or the tenderloin may be sliced into individual portions prior to wrapping and baking."@en .
