<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Doughnut> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **doughnut** or **donut** () is a type of food made from leavened [fried dough](:List_of_fried_dough_foods). It is popular in many countries and is prepared in various forms as a sweet snack that can be homemade or purchased in [bakeries](:Bakery), [supermarkets](:Supermarket), [food stalls](:Food_booth), and [franchised specialty vendors](:List_of_doughnut_shops). *Doughnut* is the traditional spelling, while *donut* is the simplified version; the terms are used interchangeably.Doughnuts are usually [deep fried](:Deep_fried) from a [flour dough](:Flour_dough), but other types of [batters](:Batter_%28cooking%29) can also be used. Various toppings and flavorings are used for different types, such as [sugar](:Sugar), [chocolate](:Chocolate) or [maple](:Maple_syrup) glazing. Doughnuts may also include water, [leavening](:Leavening_agent), eggs, milk, sugar, oil, [shortening](:Shortening), and natural or artificial flavors.The two most common types are the **ring doughnut** and the **filled doughnut**, which is injected with [fruit preserves](:Fruit_preserves) (the [jelly doughnut](:Jelly_doughnut%29), [cream](:Cream), [custard](:Custard), or other sweet fillings. Small pieces of dough are sometimes cooked as **doughnut holes**. Once fried, doughnuts may be glazed with a sugar icing, spread with icing or chocolate, or topped with [powdered sugar](:Powdered_sugar), [cinnamon](:Cinnamon), [sprinkles](:Sprinkles) or fruit. [Other shapes](:List_of_doughnut_varieties) include balls, flattened spheres, twists, and other forms. Doughnut varieties are also divided into cake (including the [old-fashioned](:Old-fashioned_doughnut%29) and yeast-risen doughnuts. Doughnuts are often accompanied by [coffee](:Coffee_and_doughnuts) or [milk](:Milk). They are sold at doughnut shops, convenience stores, petrol/gas stations, cafes or fast food restaurants."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Winged_bean> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/family> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Phaseoleae> .
