<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Tomato_sauce> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Tomato sauce** (also known as ***salsa roja*** in Spanish or ***salsa di pomodoro*** in Italian) can refer to many different sauces made primarily from [tomatoes](:Tomato), usually to be served as part of a [dish](:Dish_%28food%29), rather than as a [condiment](:Condiment). Tomato sauces are common for [meat](:Meat) and [vegetables](:Vegetable), but they are perhaps best known as bases for sauces for Mexican [salsas](:Salsa_%28sauce%29) and Italian [pasta dishes](:Pasta_dish). Tomatoes have a rich flavor, high water content, soft flesh which breaks down easily, and the right composition to thicken into a sauce when stewed without the need of [thickeners](:Thickeners) such as [roux](:Roux) or [masa](:Masa). All of these qualities make them ideal for simple and appealing sauces.In countries such as the United Kingdom, India, Australia, New Zealand, and South Africa, the term *tomato sauce* is used to describe a [condiment](:Condiment) similar to what Americans call [tomato ketchup](:Ketchup). In some of these countries, both terms are used for the condiment."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
