<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Zōni> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Zōni** (雑煮 or ぞうに), often with the [honorific](:Honorific_speech_in_Japanese) \"o-\" as *o-zōni*, is a Japanese [soup](:Soup) containing *[mochi](:Mochi_%28food%29)* rice cakes. The dish is strongly associated with the [Japanese New Year](:Japanese_New_Year) and its tradition of *[osechi](:Osechi)* ceremonial foods. The preparation of zōni varies both by household and region."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Maharashtra> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/motto> "Pratipaccandralēkhēva vardhiṣṇurviśva vanditā Mahārāṣṭrasya rājyasya mudrā bhadrāya rājatē"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Candied_fruit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Candied fruit**, also known as **glacé fruit**, is whole [fruit](:Fruit), smaller pieces of fruit, or pieces of [peel](:Peel_%28fruit%29), placed in heated [sugar](:Sugar) [syrup](:Syrup), which absorbs the moisture from within the fruit and eventually [preserves](:Food_preservation) it. Depending on the size and type of fruit, this process of preservation can take from several days to several months. This process allows the fruit to retain its quality for up to a year. It has existed since the 14th century.The continual process of drenching the fruit in syrup causes the fruit to become saturated with [sugar](:Sugar), preventing the growth of [spoilage microorganisms](:Food_microbiology) due to the unfavourable [osmotic pressure](:Osmotic_pressure) this creates.Fruits that are commonly candied include [dates](:Phoenix_dactylifera), [cherries](:Cherry), [pineapple](:Pineapple), peaches, as well as [ginger root](:Ginger). The principal candied peels are [orange](:Orange_%28fruit%29) and [citron](:Citron); these with candied [lemon](:Lemon) peel are the usual ingredients of mixed chopped peel (which may also include glacé cherries).Recipes vary from region to region, but the general principle is to boil the fruit, [steep](:Steeping) it in increasingly stronger sugar solutions for a number of weeks, and then dry off any remaining water."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
