<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Stew> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **stew** is a combination of solid [food](:Food) [ingredients](:Ingredient) that have been [cooked](:Cooking) in [liquid](:Soup) and served in the resultant [gravy](:Gravy). A stew needs to have raw ingredients added to the gravy. Ingredients in a stew can include any combination of [vegetables](:Vegetable) and may include [meat](:Meat), especially tougher meats suitable for slow-cooking, such as [beef](:Beef), [pork](:Pork), [lamb](:Lamb_and_mutton), [poultry](:Poultry), [sausages](:Sausage), and [seafood](:Seafood). While water can be used as the stew-cooking liquid, [stock](:Stock_%28food%29) is also common. A small amount of red wine is sometimes added for flavour. [Seasoning](:Seasoning) and [flavourings](:Flavouring) may also be added. Stews are typically cooked at a relatively low temperature ([simmered](:Simmering), not [boiled](:Boiling%29), allowing flavours to mingle.Stewing is suitable for the least tender cuts of meat that become tender and juicy with the slow moist heat method. This makes it popular in low-cost cooking. Cuts having a certain amount of marbling and gelatinous connective tissue give moist, juicy stews, while lean meat may easily become dry.Stews are thickened by reduction or with [flour](:Flour), either by coating pieces of meat with flour before searing, or by using a [roux](:Roux) or *[beurre manié](:Beurre_mani%C3%A9)*, a dough consisting of equal parts of fat and flour. Thickeners like [cornstarch](:Cornstarch), [potato starch](:Potato_starch), or [arrowroot](:Arrowroot) may also be used.Stews are similar to [soups](:Soup), and in some cases there may not be a clear distinction between the two. Generally, stews have less liquid than soups, are much thicker and require longer cooking over low heat. While soups are almost always served in a bowl, stews may be thick enough to be served on a plate with the gravy as a sauce over the solid ingredients."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
