<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Cookie> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **cookie** is a [baked](:Baked_goods) or cooked snack or [dessert](:Dessert) that is typically small, flat and sweet. It usually contains [flour](:Flour), [sugar](:Sugar), egg, and some type of [oil](:Cooking_oil), [fat](:Fat), or [butter](:Butter). It may include other ingredients such as [raisins](:Raisin), [oats](:Oat), [chocolate chips](:Chocolate_chip), nuts, etc.Most [English-speaking countries](:English-speaking_world) call crunchy cookies **[biscuits](:Biscuits)**, except for the United States and Canada, where [biscuit](:Biscuit_%28bread%29) refers to a type of [quick bread](:Quick_bread). Chewier biscuits are sometimes called *cookies* even in the United Kingdom. Some cookies may also be named by their shape, such as [date squares](:Date_squares) or bars.Biscuit or cookie variants include [sandwich biscuits](:Sandwich_cookie), such as [custard creams](:Custard_cream), [Jammie Dodgers](:Jammie_Dodgers), [Bourbons](:Bourbon_biscuit) and [Oreos](:Oreo), with marshmallow or jam filling and sometimes dipped in [chocolate](:Chocolate) or another sweet coating. Cookies are often served with [beverages](:Drink) such as [milk](:Milk), [coffee](:Coffee) or [tea](:Tea) and sometimes [\"dunked\"](:Dunking_%28biscuit%29), an approach which releases more [flavour](:Flavoring) from [confections](:Confectionery) by dissolving the sugars, while also softening their texture. Factory-made cookies are sold in [grocery stores](:Grocery_store), [convenience stores](:Convenience_store) and [vending machines](:Vending_machine). Fresh-baked cookies are sold at [bakeries](:Bakery) and [coffeehouses](:Coffeehouse)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
