<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Maple_syrup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Maple syrup** is a [syrup](:Syrup) made from the [sap](:Sap) of [maple](:Maple) trees. In cold climates, these trees store [starch](:Starch) in their trunks and roots before winter; the starch is then converted to [sugar](:Sugar) that rises in the sap in late winter and early spring. Maple trees are tapped by drilling holes into their trunks and collecting the sap, which is processed by heating to evaporate much of the water, leaving the concentrated syrup.Maple syrup was first made by the [Indigenous peoples of North America](:Indigenous_peoples_of_North_America). The practice was adopted by European settlers, who gradually changed production methods. Technological improvements in the 1970s further refined syrup processing. Virtually all of the world's maple syrup is produced in Canada and the United States. The Canadian province of [Quebec](:Quebec) is the largest producer, responsible for 70 percent of the world's output; Canadian exports of maple syrup in 2016 were [C$](:Canadian_dollar)487 million (about [US$](:United_States_dollar)360 million), with Quebec accounting for some 90 percent of this total.Maple syrup is graded based on its colour and taste. [Sucrose](:Sucrose) is the most prevalent sugar in maple syrup. In Canada, syrups must be made exclusively from maple sap to qualify as maple syrup and must also be at least 66 percent sugar. In the United States, a syrup must be made almost entirely from maple sap to be labelled as \"maple\", though states such as [Vermont](:Vermont) and [New York](:New_York_%28state%29) have more restrictive definitions.Maple syrup is often used as a condiment for [pancakes](:Pancake), [waffles](:Waffle), [French toast](:French_toast), [oatmeal](:Oatmeal), or [porridge](:Porridge). It is also used as an ingredient in [baking](:Baking) and as a sweetener or flavouring agent. Culinary experts have praised its unique flavour, although the chemistry responsible is not fully understood."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Poland> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/Place> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Junay_(food)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Junay or junai, is a Filipino packed rice dish wrapped in banana leaves with burnt coconut meat and various spices. It originates from the Tausug people of the Sulu Archipelago. It is made by boiling rice in coconut milk until half-cooked. It is then wrapped in banana leaves with pamapa (powdered mixed spices), oil, salt, and siyunog lahing (powdered burnt coconut meat). It is further steamed in water until fully cooked. The spices and burnt coconut are also sold pre-mixed and are known as pipis itum."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ovos_moles> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Ovos moles de Aveiro** (literally, \"soft eggs from [Aveiro](:Aveiro%2C_Portugal)\")—sometimes written as **ovos-moles de Aveiro**—are a local [pastry](:Pastry) [delicacy](:Delicacy) from [Aveiro District](:Aveiro_District), [Portugal](:Portugal), made of [egg yolks](:Egg_yolk) and [sugar](:Sugar), and sometimes [chocolate](:Chocolate). This mixture is then put inside small [rice paper](:Rice_paper) casings or [wheat flour](:Wheat_flour) casings similar to [communion wafers](:Communion_wafer) shaped into nautical shapes such as shells.In 2008, it was designated a product with [Protected Geographical Indication](:Protected_Geographical_Indication) by the European Union."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Salzburger_Nockerl> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Salzburger Nockerl (pl., Austro-Bavarian: Soizbuaga Noggal) are a sweet soufflé served as a dessert, a culinary specialty in the Austrian city of Salzburg."@en .
