<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ayam_penyet> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Ayam penyet** ([Javanese](:Javanese_language) for smashed fried chicken) is [Indonesian](:Indonesian_cuisine) — more precisely [East](:East_Java) [Javanese cuisine](:Javanese_cuisine) — fried chicken dish consisting of fried chicken that is smashed with the pestle against the [mortar](:Mortar_and_pestle) to make it softer, and is served with [sambal](:Sambal), slices of [cucumbers](:Cucumber), [fried tofu](:Fried_tofu), and [tempeh](:Tempeh). In Indonesia, *penyet* dishes such as fried chicken and ribs are commonly associated with [Surabaya](:Surabaya), the capital city of [East Java](:East_Java). The most popular ayam penyet variant is ayam penyet Suroboyo.*Ayam penyet* is known for its spicy sambal, which is made with a mixture of [chilli](:Chili_pepper), [anchovies](:Anchovy_%28food%29), [tomatoes](:Tomato), [shallots](:Shallot), [garlic](:Garlic), [shrimp paste](:Shrimp_paste), [tamarind](:Tamarind) and [lime juice](:Lime_juice). Like its namesake, the sambal mixture is then smashed into a paste to be eaten with the dish.*Penyet* is a [Javanese](:Javanese_language) term for \"squeezed\" or \"pressed,\" thus *ayam penyet* means \"squeezed chicken.\" It is quite similar to another popular [Indonesian fried chicken](:Ayam_goreng) dish *[ayam geprek](:Ayam_geprek)*, as both are fried chicken smashed and mixed together with hot and spicy *[sambal](:Sambal)* chili paste. The difference is *ayam penyet* is a traditional Javanese *[ayam goreng](:Ayam_goreng)* half-cooked in *[bumbu kuning](:Bumbu_%28seasoning%29)* (yellow spice paste) and then [deep fried](:Deep_frying) in hot [palm oil](:Palm_oil). *Ayam geprek* however, is more akin to western-style (American) [fried chicken](:Fried_chicken), which is crispy fried chicken coated with [batter](:Batter_%28cooking%29), or known in Indonesia as *ayam goreng tepung* (battered fried chicken).Today ayam penyet is commonly found in [Indonesia](:Indonesia), [Malaysia](:Malaysia), [Brunei](:Brunei) and [Singapore](:Singapore). It has recently surged in popularity across [Southeast Asia](:Southeast_Asia), where various chains of franchises have opened selling the dish along with other Indonesian delicacies."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
