<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Sandwich> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **sandwich** is a food typically consisting of vegetables, sliced cheese or meat, placed on or between slices of bread, or more generally any dish wherein bread serves as a container or wrapper for another food type. The sandwich began as a portable, convenient [finger food](:Finger_food) in the Western world, though over time it has become prevalent worldwide.In the 21st century there has been considerable debate over the precise definition of *sandwich*; and specifically whether a [hot dog](:Hot_dog) or [open sandwich](:Open_sandwich) can be categorized as such. In the United States, the [Department of Agriculture](:United_States_Department_of_Agriculture) and the [Food and Drug Administration](:Food_and_Drug_Administration) are the responsible agencies. The USDA uses the definition, \"at least 35% cooked meat and no more than 50% bread\" for closed sandwiches, and \"at least 50% cooked meat\" for open sandwiches. In Britain, the [British Sandwich Association](:British_Sandwich_Association) defines a sandwich as \"any form of bread with a filling, generally assembled cold\", a definition which includes wraps and bagels, but excludes dishes assembled and served hot, such as [burgers](:Hamburger).Sandwiches are a popular type of lunch food, taken to work, school, or [picnics](:Picnic) to be eaten as part of a [packed lunch](:Packed_lunch). The bread may be plain or be coated with [condiments](:Condiment), such as [mayonnaise](:Mayonnaise) or [mustard](:Mustard_%28condiment%29), to enhance its flavour and texture. As well as being homemade, sandwiches are also widely sold in various retail outlets and can be served hot or cold. There are both savoury sandwiches, such as [deli meat](:Deli_meat) sandwiches, and sweet sandwiches, such as a [peanut butter and jelly sandwich](:Peanut_butter_and_jelly_sandwich).The sandwich is named after its supposed inventor, [John Montagu, 4th Earl of Sandwich](:John_Montagu%2C_4th_Earl_of_Sandwich). *[The Wall Street Journal](:The_Wall_Street_Journal)* has described it as Britain's \"biggest contribution to [gastronomy](:Gastronomy)\"."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
