<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Chicken_as_food> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "[Chicken](:Chicken) is the most common type of [poultry](:Poultry) in the world. Owing to the relative ease and low cost of raising chickens—in comparison to mammals such as [cattle](:Cattle) or [hogs](:Pig)—chicken [meat](:Meat) (commonly called just \"chicken\") and chicken [eggs](:Chicken_eggs) have become prevalent in numerous [cuisines](:Cuisines).Chicken can be prepared in a vast range of ways, including [baking](:Baking), [grilling](:Grilling), [barbecuing](:Barbecuing), [frying](:Frying), and [boiling](:Boiling). Since the latter half of the 20th century, prepared chicken has become a staple of [fast food](:Fast_food). Chicken is sometimes cited as being more healthful than [red meat](:Red_meat), with lower concentrations of [cholesterol](:Cholesterol) and [saturated fat](:Saturated_fat).The [poultry farming](:Poultry_farming) industry that accounts for chicken production takes on a range of forms across different parts of the world. In [developed countries](:Developed_country), chickens are typically subject to [intensive farming](:Intensive_farming) methods while less-developed areas raise chickens using more traditional farming techniques. The [United Nations](:United_Nations) estimates there to be 19 billion chickens on Earth today, making them outnumber humans more than two to one."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
