<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Khuushuur> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Khuushuur** (Template:Lang-mn Template:IPA-mn; [Chinese](:Simplified_Chinese_characters): [火烧儿](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/火烧儿); [pinyin](:Pinyin): *huǒshāor*; Russian: чебуре́к, [romanized](:Romanization_of_Russian): *cheburek*,  ) is a meat pastry or [dumpling](:Dumpling) popular in [Mongolia](:Mongolia) that is relatively similar to similar items in [Russian](:Russian_cuisine) and other cuisines like [chiburekki](:Chiburekki). The meat, [beef](:Beef), [mutton](:Lamb_and_mutton), or [camel](:Camel), is ground up and mixed with [onion](:Onion) (or [garlic](:Garlic%29), salt and other spices. The cook rolls the dough into circles, then places the meat inside the dough and folds the dough in half, creating a flat half-circular pocket. The cook then closes the pockets by pressing the edges together. A variety of khuushuur has a round shape produced by pressing the dough and mince together using the dough roller.After making the pockets, the cook fries them in oil until the dough turns a golden brown. The khuushuur is then served hot, and can be eaten by hand.This type of [Mongolian cuisine](:Cuisine_of_Mongolia) is similar to [buuz](:Buuz) in that the meat is prepared in the same way and cooked in a dough pocket, the principal difference being that buuz is steamed instead of fried."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
