<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Yogurt> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Yogurt** (; , from Template:Lang-tr, also spelled **yoghurt**, **yogourt** or **yoghourt**) is a food produced by bacterial [fermentation](:Fermentation_%28food%29) of milk. The bacteria used to make yogurt are known as *yogurt cultures*. Fermentation of sugars in the milk by these bacteria produces [lactic acid](:Lactic_acid), which acts on [milk protein](:Milk_protein) to give yogurt its [texture](:Texture_%28food%29) and characteristic tart flavor. Cow's milk is the milk most commonly used to make yogurt. Milk from [water buffalo](:Water_buffalo), goats, [ewes](:Sheep), [mares](:Mare), camels, and [yaks](:Yak) are also used to produce yogurt. The milk used may be [homogenized](:Milk%23Creaming_and_homogenization) or not. It may be [pasteurized](:Pasteurized) or [raw](:Raw_milk). Each type of milk produces substantially different results.Yogurt is produced using a culture of [*Lactobacillus delbrueckii* subsp. *bulgaricus*](:Lactobacillus_delbrueckii_subsp%2E_bulgaricus) and *[Streptococcus thermophilus](:Streptococcus_thermophilus)* bacteria. In addition, other [lactobacilli](:Lactobacillus) and [bifidobacteria](:Bifidobacterium) are sometimes added during or after culturing yogurt. Some countries require yogurt to contain a specific amount of [colony-forming units](:Colony-forming_unit) (CFU) of bacteria; in China, for example, the requirement for the number of lactobacillus bacteria is at least 1 million CFU per milliliter.To produce yogurt, milk is first heated, usually to about 85 °C (185 °F), to [denature](:Denaturation_%28biochemistry%29) the milk proteins so that they do not form [curds](:Curd). After heating, the milk is allowed to cool to about 45 °C (113 °F). The bacterial culture is mixed in, and a warm temperature of 30–45 °C (86–113 °F) is maintained for 4 to 12 hours to allow fermentation to occur, with the higher temperatures working faster but risking a lumpy texture or whey separation."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
