<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Fried_rice> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Fried rice** is a dish of [cooked rice](:Cooked_rice) that has been [stir-fried](:Stir_frying) in a [wok](:Wok) or a [frying pan](:Frying_pan) and is usually mixed with other ingredients such as [eggs](:Egg_%28food%29), [vegetables](:Vegetable), [seafood](:Seafood), or [meat](:Meat). It is often eaten by itself or as an accompaniment to another dish. Fried rice is a popular component of [East Asian](:East_Asian_cuisine), [Southeast Asian](:Southeast_Asian_cuisine) and certain [South Asian cuisines](:South_Asian_cuisine), as well as a staple [national dish](:National_dish) of [Indonesia](:Indonesia). As a homemade dish, fried rice is typically made with ingredients [left over from other dishes](:Leftovers), leading to countless variations. Fried rice first developed during the [Sui Dynasty](:Sui_Dynasty) in [China](:China) and as such all fried rice dishes can trace their origins to [Chinese fried rice](:Chinese_fried_rice).Many varieties of fried rice have their own specific list of ingredients. In Greater China, common varieties include [Yangzhou fried rice](:Yangzhou_fried_rice) and [Hokkien fried rice](:Hokkien_fried_rice). Japanese [chāhan](:Chahan_%28dish%29) is considered a [Japanese Chinese](:Japanese_Chinese_cuisine) dish, having derived from Chinese fried rice dishes. In Southeast Asia, similarly constructed [Indonesian](:Indonesian_language), [Malaysian](:Malaysian_Chinese), and [Singaporean](:Singaporeans) *[nasi goreng](:Nasi_goreng)* and Thai *[khao phat](:Khao_phat)* are popular dishes. In the West, most restaurants catering to vegetarians have invented their own varieties of fried rice, including egg fried rice. Fried rice is also seen on the menus of American restaurants offering cuisines with no native tradition of the dish. Additionally, the cuisine of some Latin American countries includes variations on fried rice, including Ecuadorian chaulafan, Peruvian [arroz chaufa](:Arroz_chaufa), Cuban arroz frito, and Puerto Rican arroz mamposteao.Fried rice is a common [street food](:Street_food) in Asia. In some Asian countries, small restaurants, street vendors and traveling hawkers specialize in serving fried rice. In Indonesian cities it is common to find fried rice street hawkers moving through the streets with their food cart and stationing it in busy streets or residential areas. Many [Southeast Asian](:Southeast_Asia) street food stands offer fried rice with a selection of optional garnishes and side dishes."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ham> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Ham is pork from a leg cut that has been preserved by wet or dry curing, with or without smoking. As a processed meat, the term \"ham\" includes both whole cuts of meat and ones that have been mechanically formed. Ham is made around the world, including a number of regional specialties, such as Westphalian ham and some varieties of Spanish jamón. In addition, numerous ham products have specific geographical naming protection, such as prosciutto di Parma in Europe, and Smithfield ham in the US."@en .
