<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Soup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Soup** is a primarily [liquid](:Liquid) [food](:Food), generally served warm or hot (but may be cool or cold), that is made by combining [ingredients](:Ingredients) of [meat](:Meat) or [vegetables](:Vegetable) with [stock](:Stock_%28food%29), milk, or water. Hot soups are additionally characterized by [boiling](:Boiling) solid [ingredients](:Ingredient) in liquids in a pot until the [flavors](:Flavor_%28taste%29) are extracted, forming a [broth](:Broth). Soups are similar to [stews](:Stew), and in some cases there may not be a clear distinction between the two; however, soups generally have more liquid (broth) than stews.In traditional French cuisine, soups are classified into two main groups: *clear soups* and *thick soups*. The established [French](:France) classifications of clear soups are *[bouillon](:Bouillon_%28broth%29)* and *[consommé](:Consomm%C3%A9)*. Thick soups are classified depending upon the type of thickening agent used: *[purées](:Pur%C3%A9e)* are vegetable soups thickened with [starch](:Starch); *[bisques](:Bisque_%28food%29)* are made from puréed [shellfish](:Shellfish) or vegetables thickened with [cream](:Cream); cream soups may be thickened with [béchamel sauce](:B%C3%A9chamel_sauce); and *[veloutés](:Velout%C3%A9_sauce)* are thickened with [eggs](:Egg_%28food%29), [butter](:Butter), and cream. Other ingredients commonly used to thicken soups and broths include [rice](:Rice), [lentils](:Lentils), [flour](:Flour), and [grains](:Grain); many popular soups also include pumpkin, carrots, potatoes, [pig's trotters](:Pig%27s_trotter) and [bird's nests](:Edible_bird%27s_nest).Other types of soup include [fruit soups](:Fruit_soup), dessert soups, pulse soups like split pea, cold soups and other styles."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Malaysia> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/PopulatedPlace> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Porridge> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Porridge is a food made by heating or boiling ground, crushed or chopped starchy plants, typically grain, in milk or water. It is often cooked or served with added flavourings such as sugar, honey, (dried) fruit or syrup to make a sweet cereal, or it can be mixed with spices, meat or vegetables to make a savoury dish. It is usually served hot in a bowl, depending on its consistency. Oat porridge, or oatmeal, is one of the most common types of porridge. Gruel is a thinner version of porridge."@en .
