<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ceviche> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Ceviche** (Template:IPA-es is a [Peruvian](:Peruvian) dish typically made from fresh raw fish [cured](:Curing_%28food_preservation%29) in fresh citrus juices, most commonly [lime](:Lime_%28fruit%29) or [lemon](:Lemon). It is also spiced with *[ají](:Capsicum_baccatum)*, [chili peppers](:Chili_pepper) or other seasonings and [julienned](:Julienning) red onions, salt, and [cilantro](:Cilantro) are also added. The name originates from the [Quechuan](:Quechua_language) word *siwichi*, which means fresh or tender fish.Because the dish is eaten raw, and not cooked with heat, it must be prepared fresh and consumed immediately to minimize the risk of [food poisoning](:Foodborne_illness). Ceviche is often eaten as an [appetizer](:Appetizer); if eaten as a main dish, it is usually accompanied by side dishes that complement its flavors, such as [sweet potato](:Sweet_potato), [lettuce](:Lettuce), [maize](:Maize), [avocado](:Avocado), or [cooking banana](:Cooking_banana).The dish is popular in the [Pacific](:Pacific_Ocean) coastal regions of western [South America](:South_America). The origin of ceviche is from the ancient [Moche culture](:Moche_culture), which today corresponds to the modern day country of [Peru](:Peru). The technique of macerating raw fish and meat in vinegar, citrus, and spices ([escabeche](:Escabeche%29) was brought to the Americas from Spain and is linked to the Muslim heritage in Spanish cuisine. However, archeological records suggest that something resembling ceviche may have been in the western South America as early as 2,000 years ago. The dominant position [Lima](:Lima) held through four centuries as the capital of the [Viceroyalty of Peru](:Viceroyalty_of_Peru) allowed for popular dishes such as ceviche to be brought to other Spanish administrative provinces in the region, and in time they became a part of local cuisine by incorporating regional flavors and styles."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
