<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Vodka> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Vodka** (Template:Lang-pl Template:IPA-pl, Template:Lang-ru Template:IPA-ru, Template:Lang-sv Template:IPA-sv is a clear [distilled alcoholic beverage](:Distilled_beverage). Different varieties originated in [Poland](:Poland), [Russia](:Russia), and [Sweden](:Sweden). Vodka is composed mainly of water and [ethanol](:Ethanol) but sometimes with traces of impurities and flavourings. Traditionally, it is made by distilling liquid from [fermented](:Fermentation_in_food_processing) [cereal grains](:Cereal), and [potatoes](:Potato) since introduced in Europe in the 1700's. Some modern brands use fruits, honey, or maple sap as the base.Since the 1890s, standard vodkas have been 40% [alcohol by volume](:Alcohol_by_volume) (ABV) (80 [U.S. proof](:U%2ES%2E_proof%29). The [European Union](:European_Union) has established a minimum alcohol content of 37.5% for vodka. Vodka in the United States must have a minimum alcohol content of 40%.Vodka is traditionally drunk \"[neat](:Bartending_terminology)\" (not mixed with water, ice, or other [mixers](:Mixer_drink%29), and it is often served *freezer chilled* in the [vodka belt](:Alcohol_belts_of_Europe%23Vodka_belt) of Belarus, Estonia, Finland, Iceland, Latvia, Lithuania, Norway, Poland, Russia, Sweden, and Ukraine. It is also used in [cocktails and mixed drinks](:Cocktail), such as the [vodka martini](:Vodka_martini), [Cosmopolitan](:Cosmopolitan_%28cocktail%29), [vodka tonic](:Vodka_tonic), [screwdriver](:Screwdriver_%28cocktail%29), [greyhound](:Greyhound_%28cocktail%29), [Black](:Black_Russian) or [White Russian](:White_Russian_%28cocktail%29), [Moscow mule](:Moscow_mule), [Bloody Mary](:Bloody_Mary_%28cocktail%29), and [Caesar](:Caesar_%28cocktail%29)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
