<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Pastry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Pastry** is [baked](:Baking) food made with a [dough](:Dough) of [flour](:Flour), [water](:Water) and [shortening](:Shortening) (solid fats, including [butter](:Butter) or [lard](:Lard%29) that may be savoury or sweetened. Sweetened pastries are often described as *[bakers' confectionery](:Flour_confections)*. The word \"pastries\" suggests many kinds of [baked products](:Baking) made from [ingredients](:Ingredients) such as [flour](:Flour), [sugar](:Sugar), [milk](:Milk), [butter](:Butter), [shortening](:Shortening), [baking powder](:Baking_powder), and [eggs](:Egg_%28food%29). Small [tarts](:Tarts) and other sweet baked products are called **pastries** as a [synecdoche](:Synecdoche). Common pastry dishes include [tarts](:Tarts), [quiches](:Quiche), [croissants](:Croissant), and [pasties](:Pasty).The French word [pâtisserie](:P%C3%A2tisserie) is also used in English (with or without the accent) for the same foods. Originally, the French word *pastisserie* referred to anything, such as a meat pie, made in dough (*paste*, later *pâte*) and not typically a luxurious or sweet product. This meaning still persisted in the nineteenth century, though by then the term more often referred to the sweet and often ornate confections implied today.Pastry can also refer to the **pastry dough**, from which such baked products are made. Pastry dough is rolled out thinly and used as a base for baked products.Pastry is differentiated from [bread](:Bread) by having a higher fat content, which contributes to a flaky or crumbly texture. A good pastry is light and airy and fatty, but firm enough to support the weight of the filling. When making a [shortcrust pastry](:Shortcrust_pastry), care must be taken to blend the fat and flour thoroughly before adding any liquid. This ensures that the flour granules are adequately coated with fat and less likely to develop [gluten](:Gluten). On the other hand, overmixing results in long gluten strands that toughen the pastry. In other types of pastry such as [Danish pastry](:Danish_pastry) and [croissants](:Croissant), the characteristic flaky texture is achieved by repeatedly rolling out a dough similar to that for [yeast](:Yeast) bread, spreading it with butter, and folding it to produce many thin layers."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
