<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Kuih> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "***Kuih*** ([Indonesian](:Indonesian_language): *[kue](:Kue)*; derived from the [Hokkien](:Hokkien_language) and [Teochew](:Teochew_language) *kueh* – [粿](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/粿)) are bite-sized [snack](:Snack) or dessert foods commonly found in [Southeast Asia](:Southeast_Asia) and [China](:China). It is a fairly broad term which may include items that would be called cakes, [cookies](:Cookie), [dumplings](:Dumpling), [pudding](:Pudding), biscuits, or [pastries](:Pastry) in English and are usually made from rice or [glutinous rice](:Glutinous_rice). In China, where the term originates from, *kueh* or *koé* ([粿](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/粿)) in the [Min Nan languages](:Southern_Min) (known as \"guo\" in [Mandarin](:Mandarin_Chinese%29) refers to snacks which are typically made from rice but can occasionally be made from other grains such as wheat. The term *kuih* is widely used in [Malaysia](:Malaysia), [Brunei](:Brunei), and [Singapore](:Singapore), *kueh* is used in Singapore and [Indonesia](:Indonesia), *kue* is used in Indonesia only, all three refer to sweet or savoury desserts.Though called by other names, one is likely to find various similar versions of kuih in neighbouring countries, such as Vietnam, Thailand, and Myanmar. For example, the colourful steamed *kue lapis* and the rich *kuih bingka ubi* are also available in Myanmar, Thailand, and Vietnam. In the [Philippines](:Philippine_cuisine), *kuih* are referred to in [Tagalog](:Tagalog_language) as *[kakanin](:Kakanin)*.Kuihs are not confined to a certain meal but can be eaten throughout the day. They are an integral part of Malaysian, Indonesian, Bruneian and Singaporean festivities such as [Hari Raya](:Eid_al-Fitr) and [Chinese New Year](:Chinese_New_Year). Many kuih are sweet, but some are savoury. In the northern states of Perlis, Kedah, Perak, and Kelantan, kuih (**kuih-muih** in [Malay](:Malay_language%29) are usually sweet. In the Southeast Peninsular states of Negeri Sembilan, Melaka and Selangor, savoury kuih can be found. Kuih are more often [steamed](:Steaming) than baked, and are thus very different in texture, flavour and appearance from Western cakes or puff pastries."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
