<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Pancake> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **pancake** (or **hot-cake**, **griddlecake**, or **flapjack**) is a flat cake, often thin and round, prepared from a [starch-based](:Starch) [batter](:Batter_%28cooking%29) that may contain eggs, milk and butter and cooked on a hot surface such as a [griddle](:Griddle) or [frying pan](:Frying_pan), often frying with oil or butter. It is a type of [batter bread](:Batter_bread). Archaeological evidence suggests that pancakes were probably eaten in prehistoric societies.The pancake's shape and structure varies worldwide. In the United Kingdom, pancakes are often [unleavened](:Leavening_agent) and resemble a [crêpe](:Cr%C3%AApe). In North America, a leavening agent is used (typically [baking powder](:Baking_powder%29) creating a thick fluffy pancake. A *crêpe* is a thin [Breton](:Brittany) pancake of French origin cooked on one or both sides in a special pan or [crepe maker](:Crepe_maker) to achieve a lacelike network of fine bubbles. A well-known variation originating from [southeast Europe](:Southeast_Europe) is a *[palačinke](:Pala%C4%8Dinke)*, a thin moist pancake fried on both sides and filled with jam, cream cheese, chocolate, or ground walnuts, but many other fillings—sweet or savoury—can also be used.Commercially prepared pancake mixes are available in some countries. Like [waffles](:Waffles), commercially prepared frozen pancakes are available from companies like [Eggo](:Eggo). When [buttermilk](:Buttermilk) is used in place of or in addition to milk, the pancake develops a tart flavor and becomes known as a **buttermilk pancake**, which is common in Scotland and the US. [Buckwheat](:Buckwheat) flour can be used in a pancake batter, making for a type of [buckwheat pancake](:Buckwheat_pancake), a category that includes [Blini](:Blini), [Kaletez](:Kaletez), [Ploye](:Ploye), and [Memil-buchimgae](:Memil-buchimgae). When potato is used as a major portion of the batter, the result is a *[potato pancake](:Potato_pancake)*.Pancakes may be served at any time of the day or year with a variety of toppings or fillings, but they have developed associations with particular times and toppings in different regions. In North America, they are typically considered a [breakfast food](:Breakfast_food) and serve a similar function to [waffles](:Waffle). In Britain and [the Commonwealth](:The_Commonwealth_of_Nations), they are associated with [Shrove Tuesday](:Shrove_Tuesday), commonly known as \"Pancake Day\", when, historically, perishable ingredients had to be used up before the fasting period of [Lent](:Lent)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
