<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Biscuit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **biscuit** is a [flour-based](:Flour) baked and shaped [food](:Food) product. In most countries biscuits are typically hard, flat, and [unleavened](:Unleavened). They are usually sweet and may be made with [sugar](:Sugar), [chocolate](:Chocolate), [icing](:Icing_%28food%29), [jam](:Jam), [ginger](:Ginger), or [cinnamon](:Cinnamon). They can also be savoury, similar to [crackers](:Cracker_%28food%29). Types of biscuit include [sandwich biscuits](:Sandwich_biscuit), [digestive biscuits](:Digestive_biscuit), [ginger biscuits](:Ginger_biscuit), [shortbread](:Shortbread) biscuits, [chocolate chip cookies](:Chocolate_chip_cookie), [chocolate-coated marshmallow treats](:Chocolate-coated_marshmallow_treats), [Anzac biscuits](:Anzac_biscuit), *[biscotti](:Biscotti)*, and *[speculaas](:Speculaas)*.In most of North America, nearly all hard sweet biscuits are called \"[cookies](:Cookie)\", while the term \"[biscuit](:Biscuit_%28bread%29)\" is used for a soft, leavened [quick bread](:Quick_bread) similar to a less sweet version of a *[scone](:Scone)*. \"Biscuit\" may also refer to hard flour-based baked [animal feed](:Animal_feed), as with [dog biscuit](:Dog_biscuit)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
