<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Bread> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Bread** is a [staple food](:Staple_food) prepared from a [dough](:Dough) of [flour](:Flour) (usually [wheat](:Wheat%29) and water, usually by [baking](:Baking). Throughout [recorded history](:Recorded_history) and around the world, it has been an important part of many cultures' diet. It is one of the oldest human-made foods, having been of significance since the dawn of [agriculture](:Agriculture%23History), and plays an essential role in both religious rituals and secular culture.Bread may be [leavened](:Leavening_agent) by naturally occurring microbes (e.g. sourdough), chemicals (e.g. baking soda), industrially produced [yeast](:Baker%27s_yeast), or high-pressure aeration, which creates the gas bubbles that fluff up bread. In many countries, commercial bread often contains additives to improve flavor, texture, color, shelf life, nutrition, and ease of production."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
