<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Bagel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **bagel** (Template:Lang-yi; Template:Lang-pl; also spelled **beigel**) is a [bread roll](:Bread_roll) originating in the [Jewish communities](:History_of_Jews_in_Poland) of [Poland](:Poland). It is traditionally shaped by hand into the form of a roughly hand-sized ring from [yeasted](:Yeast) [wheat](:Wheat) dough that is first [boiled](:Boiling_in_cooking) for a short time in water and then [baked](:Baking). The result is a dense, chewy, doughy interior with a browned and sometimes crisp exterior. Bagels are often topped with seeds baked on the outer crust, with the traditional ones being [poppy](:Poppy) and [sesame](:Sesame) seeds. Some may have [salt](:Salt) sprinkled on their surface, and there are different dough types, such as whole-grain and rye. The basic roll-with-a-hole design is hundreds of years old and has other practical advantages besides providing more even cooking and baking of the dough: The hole could be used to thread string or dowels through groups of bagels, allowing easier handling and transportation and more appealing seller displays.The earliest known mention of a boiled-then-baked ring-shaped bread can be found in a 13th-century Syrian cookbook, where they are referred to as [ka'ak](:Ka%27ak). Bagels have been widely associated with [Ashkenazi Jews](:Ashkenazi_Jews) since the 17th century; they were first mentioned in 1610 in Jewish community ordinances in [Kraków](:Krak%C3%B3w), Poland. Bagel-like bread known as [obwarzanek](:Obwarzanek_krakowski) was common earlier in Poland as seen in royal family accounts from 1394.Bagels are now a popular bread product in North America and Poland, especially in cities with a large [Jewish](:Jews) population, many with alternative ways of making them. Bagels are also sold (fresh or frozen, often in many flavors) in supermarkets."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
