<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ketchup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Ketchup** or **catsup** is a [table condiment](:Table_condiment) with a sweet and tangy flavor. The unmodified term (\"ketchup\") now typically refers to **tomato ketchup**, although early recipes used [egg whites](:Egg_white), [mushrooms](:Mushroom_ketchup), [oysters](:Oyster), [grapes](:Grape), [mussels](:Mussel), or [walnuts](:Walnut), among other ingredients.Tomato ketchup is made from [tomatoes](:Tomato), sugar, and [vinegar](:Vinegar), with [seasonings](:Seasoning) and [spices](:Spice). The spices and flavors vary, but commonly include [onions](:Onion), [allspice](:Allspice), [coriander](:Coriander_seed), [cloves](:Cloves), [cumin](:Cumin), [garlic](:Garlic), and [mustard](:Mustard_seed), and sometimes include [celery](:Celery), [cinnamon](:Cinnamon), or [ginger](:Ginger). The market leader in the United States (60% market share) and the [United Kingdom](:United_Kingdom) (82%) is [Heinz Tomato Ketchup](:Heinz_Tomato_Ketchup). Tomato ketchup is most often used as a condiment to dishes that are usually served hot and are fried or greasy: [french fries](:French_fries) and other [potato dishes](:List_of_potato_dishes), [hamburgers](:Hamburger), [hot dogs](:Hot_dog), [chicken tenders](:Chicken_tender), [hot sandwiches](:Hot_sandwich), [meat pies](:Meat_pie), cooked [eggs](:Egg_as_food), and grilled or fried meat. Ketchup is sometimes used as the basis for, or as one ingredient in, other sauces and dressings, and the flavor may be replicated as an [additive flavoring](:Flavoring) for snacks, such as [potato chips](:Potato_chip)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Okra> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Okra or Okro (US: /ˈoʊkrə/, UK: /ˈɒkrə/), Abelmoschus esculentus, known in many English-speaking countries as ladies' fingers or ochro, is a flowering plant in the mallow family. It has edible green seed pods. The geographical origin of okra is disputed, with supporters of West African, Ethiopian, Southeast Asian, and South Asian origins. Cultivated in tropical, subtropical, and warm temperate regions around the world, okra is used in the cuisines of many countries."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Gatsby_(sandwich)> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name> "Gatsby"@en .
