<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Pretzel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **pretzel** (), from German pronunciation, standard Template:Lang-de ( and [French](:French_language) / [Alsatian](:Alsatian_dialect): *Bretzel*) is a type of [baked](:Baking) bread made from [dough](:Dough) that is commonly shaped into a [knot](:Knot). The traditional pretzel shape is a distinctive symmetrical form, with the ends of a long strip of dough intertwined and then twisted back onto itself in a particular way (a pretzel loop or pretzel bow). Today, pretzels come in a wide range of shapes.[Salt](:Salt) is the most common seasoning, or topping, for pretzels, complementing the [washing soda](:Sodium_carbonate) or [lye](:Sodium_hydroxide) [treatment](:Lye_roll) that gives pretzels their traditional skin and flavor acquired through the [Maillard reaction](:Maillard_reaction). Other seasonings are [mustard](:Mustard_%28condiment%29), [cheeses](:Cheese), [sugar](:Sugar), [chocolate](:Chocolate), [cinnamon](:Cinnamon), [sweet glazing](:Glaze_%28cooking_technique%29), [seeds](:Seed), and [nuts](:Nut_%28fruit%29). Varieties of pretzels include soft pretzels, which should be eaten shortly after preparation, and hard-baked pretzels, which have a long shelf life."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
