<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Gofio> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Gofio** is a sort of [Canarian](:Canarian) [flour](:Flour) made from [roasted](:Roasting) grains (typically [wheat](:Wheat) or certain varieties of [maize](:Maize%29) or other starchy plants (e.g. [beans](:Bean) and, historically, [fern](:Fern) root), some varieties containing a little added [salt](:Salt). Gofio has been an important ingredient in Canarian cooking for some time, and Canarian [emigrants](:Emigrant) have spread its use to the [Caribbean](:Caribbean) (notably in [Cuba](:Cuba), [Dominican Republic](:Dominican_Republic), [Puerto Rico](:Puerto_Rico), and [Venezuela](:Venezuela%29) and the [Western Sahara](:Western_Sahara). There are various ways to use it, such as kneading, dissolving in soup, and baking. It can also be used as a thickener. It is also found in [Argentina](:Argentina), [Uruguay](:Uruguay), and [Chile](:Chile), where it is known as *harina tostada* and is employed in a wide variety of recipes. The gofio commercially available in the Canary Islands is always finely ground, like ordinary flour, despite the definition given in the [Spanish](:Spanish_language) *[Dictionary of the Royal Academy](:Diccionario_de_la_lengua_espa%C3%B1ola_de_la_Real_Academia_Espa%C3%B1ola)*. It can't be seen at shops other than the Canary Islands.In 2014, the name *Gofio Canario* was added to the register of [Protected designation of origin](:Protected_designation_of_origin) and [Protected geographical indication](:Protected_geographical_indication) by the [European Commission](:European_Commission)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/State_of_Palestine> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Nations> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Neobiani> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "***Neobiani*** (너비아니) or ***nubianie*** refers to thinly sliced, marinated, and grilled beef. In [Korean](:Korean_language), the word *neobiani* means that the beef is sliced into broad sections."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
