<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Chaoqi> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Chaoqi (Chinese: 炒琪/炒祺) is a traditional Chinese snack. It is the dough pieces covered with Guanyin Clay, a kind of soil. The primary raw materials for making Chaoqi are flour, edible oil, egg, sugar, salt, and sesame. It has various flavors like milk flavor, sesame flavor, and five spices flavor."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Poland__HistoricalEvent__7> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Breakfast> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Breakfast is the first meal of the day usually eaten in the morning. The word in English refers to breaking the fasting period of the previous night. Various \"typical\" or \"traditional\" breakfast menus exist, with food choices varying by regions and traditions worldwide."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom__HistoricalEvent__1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1656682> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Fried_chicken> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Fried chicken**, also known as **Southern fried chicken**, is a dish consisting of [chicken](:Chicken_%28food%29) pieces that have been coated with seasoned flour or [batter](:Batter_%28cooking%29) and [pan-fried](:Pan_frying), [deep fried](:Deep_frying), [pressure fried](:Pressure_frying), or [air fried](:Air_fryer). The [breading](:Breading) adds a crisp coating or crust to the exterior of the chicken while retaining juices in the meat. [Broiler](:Broiler) chickens are most commonly used.The first dish known to have been deep fried was [fritters](:Fritters), which were popular in the European [Middle Ages](:Middle_Ages). However, the [Scottish](:Scottish_people) were the first Europeans to deep fry their chicken in fat (though without seasoning). Meanwhile, many West African peoples had traditions of seasoned fried chicken (though battering and cooking the chicken in [palm oil](:Palm_oil%29). Scottish frying techniques and West African seasoning techniques were combined by enslaved Africans and African Americans in the [American South](:American_South)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
