<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Corn_starch> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Corn starch**, **maize starch**, or **cornflour** ([British English](:British_English%29) is the [starch](:Starch) derived from corn ([maize](:Maize%29) grain. The starch is obtained from the [endosperm](:Endosperm) of the [kernel](:Seed). Corn starch is a common food ingredient, often used to thicken [sauces](:Sauce) or [soups](:Soup), and to make [corn syrup](:Corn_syrup) and other [sugars](:Sugar). Corn starch is versatile, easily modified, and finds many uses in industry such as [adhesives](:Adhesive), in paper products, as an anti-sticking agent, and [textile](:Textile) manufacturing. It has medical uses as well, such as to supply glucose for people with [glycogen storage disease](:Glycogen_storage_disease).Like many products in dust form, it can be hazardous in large quantities due to its [flammability](:Flammable)—see [dust explosion](:Dust_explosion). When mixed with a fluid, corn starch can rearrange itself into a [non-Newtonian fluid](:Non-Newtonian_fluid). For example, adding water transforms corn starch into a material commonly known as [oobleck](:Non-Newtonian_fluid%23Oobleck) while adding oil transforms corn starch into an [electrorheological (ER) fluid](:Electrorheological_fluid). The concept can be explained through the mixture termed \"cornflour slime\"."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
