<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Mont_(food)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "In the [Burmese language](:Burmese_language), the term ***mont*** (Template:Lang-my; Template:IPA-my translates to \"snack\", and refers to a wide variety of prepared foods, ranging from sweet desserts to savory food items that may be cooked by steaming, baking, frying, deep-frying, or boiling. Foods made from [wheat](:Wheat_flour) or [rice flour](:Rice_flour) are generally called *mont*, but the term may also refer to certain varieties of noodle dishes, such as *[mohinga](:Mohinga)*. Burmese *mont* are typically eaten with tea during breakfast or afternoon tea time.Each variety of *mont* is designated by a descriptive word or phrase that precedes or follows the word *mont*, such as *[htoe mont](:Htoe_mont)* (lit. 'snack that is prodded') or *[mont lone yay baw](:Mont_lone_yay_baw)* (lit. 'floating snack balls'). The term *mont* has been borrowed into several regional languages, including into [Shan](:Shan_language) as [မုၼ်း](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/မုၼ်း) and into [Jingpho](:Jingpho_language) as [muk](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/muk).In Burmese, the term *mont* is not limited to [Burmese cuisine](:Burmese_cuisine): it applies equally to items as varied as Western-style [breads](:Bread) (Template:My or *paung mont*), Chinese [moon cakes](:Moon_cake) (Template:My or *la mont*), [ice cream](:Ice_cream) (Template:My or *yay ge mont*) and [tinned biscuits](:Biscuit_tin) (Template:My or *mont thitta*)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
