<http://dbpedia.org/resource/French_fries> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**French fries** ([North American English](:North_American_English%29), **chips** ([British English](:British_English%29), **finger chips** ([Indian English](:Indian_English%29), **french-fried potatoes**, or simply **fries**, are *[batonnet](:List_of_culinary_knife_cuts%23Batonnet)* or *[allumette](:Allumette)*-cut [deep-fried](:Deep_frying) [potatoes](:Potato) of disputed origin from Belgium and France. They are prepared by cutting potatoes into even strips, drying them, and frying them, usually in a [deep fryer](:Deep_fryer). Pre-cut, blanched, and frozen [russet potatoes](:Russet_potato) are widely used, and sometimes baked in a regular or [convection oven](:Convection_oven); [air fryers](:Air_fryer) are small convection ovens marketed for frying potatoes.French fries are served hot, either soft or crispy, and are generally eaten as part of lunch or dinner or by themselves as a snack, and they commonly appear on the menus of [diners](:Diner), [fast food](:Fast_food) restaurants, pubs, and bars. They are often salted and may be served with [ketchup](:Ketchup), [vinegar](:Vinegar), [mayonnaise](:Mayonnaise), [tomato sauce](:Tomato_sauce), or other local specialities. Fries can be topped more heavily, as in the dishes of [poutine](:Poutine) or [chili cheese fries](:Chili_cheese_fries). French fries can be made from [sweet potatoes](:Sweet_potato) instead of potatoes. A baked variant, oven fries, uses less or no oil."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
