<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Custard> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Custard** is a variety of culinary preparations based on sweetened [milk](:Milk), cheese, or [cream](:Cream) cooked with egg or [egg yolk](:Egg_yolk) to thicken it, and sometimes also [flour](:Flour), [corn starch](:Corn_starch), or [gelatin](:Gelatin). Depending on the [recipe](:Recipe), custard may vary in consistency from a thin pouring [sauce](:Sauce) (*[crème anglaise](:Cr%C3%A8me_anglaise)*) to the thick pastry cream (*crème pâtissière*) used to fill [éclairs](:%C3%89clair). The most common custards are used in [custard desserts](:List_of_custard_desserts) or [dessert sauces](:Dessert_sauce) and typically include [sugar](:Sugar) and [vanilla](:Vanilla); however, [savory](:Umami) custards are also found, e.g., in [quiche](:Quiche).Custard is usually cooked in a double boiler ([bain-marie](:Bain-marie%29), or heated very gently in a saucepan on a stove, though custard can also be steamed, baked in the oven with or without a [water bath](:Bain-marie), or even cooked in a [pressure cooker](:Pressure_cooking). Custard preparation is a delicate operation, because a temperature increase of 3–6 °C (5–10 °F) leads to overcooking and [curdling](:Curdling). Generally, a fully cooked custard should not exceed 80 °C (~175 °F); it begins setting at 70 °C (~160 °F). A water bath slows heat transfer and makes it easier to remove the custard from the oven before it curdles. Adding a small amount of cornflour to the egg-sugar mixture stabilises the resulting custard, allowing it to be cooked in a single pan as well as in a double-boiler. A [sous-vide](:Sous-vide) water bath may be used to precisely control temperature."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
