<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Pie> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A **pie** is a [baked](:Baking) dish which is usually made of a [pastry](:Pastry) dough casing that contains a filling of various [sweet](:Sweetness) or [savoury](:Savoury_taste) ingredients. Sweet pies may be filled with fruit (as in an [apple pie](:Apple_pie%29), nuts ([pecan pie](:Pecan_pie%29), brown sugar ([sugar pie](:Sugar_pie%29), sweetened vegetables ([rhubarb pie](:Rhubarb_pie%29), or with thicker fillings based on eggs and dairy (as in [custard pie](:Custard_pie) and [cream pie](:Cream_pie%29). Savoury pies may be filled with meat (as in a [steak pie](:Steak_pie) or a [Jamaican patty](:Jamaican_patty%29), eggs and cheese ([quiche](:Quiche%29) or a mixture of meat and vegetables ([pot pie](:Pot_pie%29).Pies are defined by their [crusts](:Crust_%28baking%29). A *filled* pie (also *single-crust* or *bottom-crust*), has pastry lining the baking dish, and the filling is placed on top of the pastry but left open. A *top-crust* pie has the filling in the bottom of the dish and is covered with a pastry or other covering before baking. A *two-crust* pie has the filling completely enclosed in the pastry shell. [Shortcrust pastry](:Shortcrust_pastry) is a typical kind of pastry used for pie crusts, but many things can be used, including [baking powder biscuits](:Biscuit_%28bread%29), [mashed potatoes](:Mashed_potatoes), and [crumbs](:Graham_cracker_crust).Pies can be a variety of sizes, ranging from bite-size to those designed for multiple servings."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
