<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Kue> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Kue** is an Indonesian bite-sized [snack](:Snack) or [dessert](:Dessert) food. Kue is a fairly broad term in [Indonesian](:Indonesian_language) to describe a wide variety of snacks including [cakes](:Cake), [cookies](:Cookie), fritters, [pies](:Pie), scones, and [patisserie](:P%C3%A2tisserie). Kue are made from a variety of ingredients in various forms; some are [steamed](:Steaming), [fried](:Frying) or [baked](:Baking). Kue are popular snacks in Indonesia, which has the largest variety of kue. Because of the countries' historical [colonial ties](:Dutch_East_Indies), *Koeé* (kue) is also popular in the [Netherlands](:Netherlands).Indonesian kue demonstrate local native delicacies, Chinese and Indian influences, as well as European cake and pastry influences. For example, [bakpia](:Bakpia) and [kue ku](:Red_tortoise_cake) are of Chinese [Peranakan](:Peranakan) origin, [kue putu](:Kue_putu) is derived from Indian [puttu](:Puttu), while [kue bugis](:Kue_bugis), [klepon](:Klepon), [nagasari](:Nagasari), [getuk](:Getuk), [lupis](:Lupis_%28food%29) and [wajik](:Wajik) are of native origin; on the other hand, [lapis legit](:Lapis_legit), [kue cubit](:Kue_cubit), [kastengel](:Kaasstengels), [risoles](:Rissole) and [pastel](:Kue_pastel) are European influenced. In [Java](:Java), traditional kue is categorized under *[jajan pasar](:Jajan_pasar)* (lit: \"market buys\" or \"market munchies\"). The colourfully decorated *jajan pasar* is usually given as a food gift, or served to accompany [tumpeng](:Tumpeng) (the main dish) during traditional Javanese ceremonies."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
