<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Shikashika> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Shikashika** is a [shaved ice](:Shaved_ice) [drink](:Drink) made from [glacier ice](:Glacier_ice) in [Peru](:Peru) and [Bolivia](:Bolivia). In some rural communities, the extraction and sale of [ice](:Ice) continues to be a primary [natural resource](:Natural_resource) for [local economies](:Local_economies). Ice merchants extract the blocks with an [axe](:Axe) by hand and transport them down from the [mountains](:Mountains) atop mules. The unit of sale is usually an ice block weighing approximately 40 [kilos](:Kilos) which is sold to Shikashika vendors. Each block sells for approximately 17 [USD](:USD). In turn, Shikashika is sold for approximately 15 cents in plastic bags with colorful sweet syrups at Andean festivals and on market day. The word Shikashika is [onomatopoeia](:Onomatopoeia) from the Amerindian language [Quechua](:Quechua_languages). An alternative word for Shikashika in [Spanish](:Spanish_language) is \"raspadilla.\""^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Sherry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Sherry (Spanish: jerez [xeˈɾeθ]) is a fortified wine made from white grapes that are grown near the city of Jerez de la Frontera in Andalusia, Spain. Sherry is produced in a variety of styles made primarily from the Palomino grape, ranging from light versions similar to white table wines, such as Manzanilla and fino, to darker and heavier versions that have been allowed to oxidise as they age in barrel, such as Amontillado and oloroso. Sweet dessert wines are also made from Pedro Ximénez or Moscatel grapes, and are sometimes blended with Palomino-based sherries."@en .
