<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Jaggery> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Jaggery** is a [traditional](:List_of_unrefined_sweeteners) [non-centrifugal cane sugar](:Non-centrifugal_cane_sugar) consumed in the [Indian Subcontinent](:Indian_subcontinent), [Southeast Asia](:Southeast_Asia), and Africa. It is a concentrated product of [cane juice](:Cane_juice) and often [date](:Date_%28fruit%29) or [palm](:Arecaceae) [sap](:Plant_sap) without separation of the [molasses](:Molasses) and crystals, and can vary from golden brown to dark brown in colour. It contains up to 50% [sucrose](:Sucrose), up to 20% [invert sugars](:Invert_sugar), and up to 20% moisture, with the remainder made up of other insoluble matter, such as [wood ash](:Wood_ash), [proteins](:Protein), and [bagasse](:Bagasse) fibres. Jaggery is very similar to [muscovado](:Muscovado), an important sweetener in [Portuguese](:Portuguese_cuisine), [British](:British_cuisine) and [French cuisine](:French_cuisine). The Kenyan Sukari ngutu/nguru has no fibre; it is dark and is made from sugar cane and also sometimes extracted from palm tree."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
